said system) the ancient readme file is indeed correct;
Provided the startup conditions, when nethack is invoked, are duplicated.

Thus;

If the player "logs on" to windows with a blank password then the files are
known by the character name pre-pended by the default user name nethack.
Thus the file names would be :-

* During game play:
    nethack-oggerella.0, nethack-oggerella.1 (etc)

* If the game is successfully saved:
    nethack-oggerella.nethack-saved-game


However, if windows has been "logged onto" prior to creating the Nethack
game, by opening Internet Explorer then, upon starting nethack, the name
by which windows knows the player shall be the file name.
Thus the file names would be :-

* During game play:
    janet.0, janet.1 (etc)

* If the game is successfully saved:
    janet-oggerella.nethack-saved-game

No doubt, this is only an issue on windows machines which allow a user
to access the system without actually logging on to windows.  And so, for
